From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] advapi32: Credential Manager keeps credentials in the person's keyring

CredWrite kept every saved credential in the user's registry hive
(Software\Wine\Credential Manager), the secret only RC4-scrambled with a
key stored right beside it: anything that could read the hive could read
every password a program had saved -- Git Credential Manager's tokens,
Remote Desktop's saved logons, mail and VPN clients'.

A credential kept beyond the logon session (CRED_PERSIST_LOCAL_MACHINE,
_ENTERPRISE) now goes to the keyring of the Unix account the program runs
as: gnome-keyring's Secret Service on the person's session bus, through
libsecret (loaded when first needed, from a new Unix side of advapi32),
encrypted at rest with their sign-in password, the same keyring their Linux
programs use. CredRead, CredEnumerate and CredDelete find it there; target
names match without regard to case, as on Windows. Each item carries the
target, type, user name, comment, alias, persistence, flags and time
written as attributes (schema org.stainedglass.Credential); the secret is
the credential blob. Writing a credential again replaces its item;
CRED_PRESERVE_CREDENTIAL_BLOB keeps its secret.

What the registry already holds moves to the keyring the first time a
program uses Credential Manager with one available: written, read back,
then removed from the hive. The registry stays the fallback -- with a
warning ("no keyring to keep credentials in") -- when there is no keyring
to use: no session bus (a service running as SYSTEM), no libsecret, no
default keyring, or a locked one. The keyring is never asked to prompt, so
a program reading a credential cannot hang on an unlock dialog. Logon-
session credentials (CRED_PERSIST_SESSION) stay in the registry's volatile
key. No secret is ever logged.

Test: test/credkeyring-gate.sh with test/credkeyring-probe.c (64- and
32-bit): secret-tool finds what CredWrite wrote, encrypted on disk, nothing
in the registry; CredRead and CredEnumerate round-trip; rewrite, preserve,
delete; migration; no-bus, locked-keyring and logon-session fallbacks.
Mutants SG_MUTANT_CRED_KEYRING and SG_MUTANT_CRED_MIGRATE
(dlls/advapi32/cred.c) each fail it.
